# Prompt Formatting Standards

Prompts are frequently reviewed and edited by human engineers. To keep them readable, diff-friendly, and token-efficient, all prompts must follow these formatting rules.

## Required Format

1. Always use triple-quoted strings with real line breaks.
2. Start and end the prompt with a line break.
3. Call .strip() to remove the leading and trailing line breaks.
4. Do not indent any prompt lines. The prompt content must start at column 0.
5. Do not use implicit string concatenation (parentheses with multiple strings).
6. Do not insert line breaks just to satisfy code line length limits. Only break lines at logical paragraph boundaries.

## Example

✅ Good:
```python
good_example = """
Prompt starts from a new line without indentation using triple quotes.

It doesn't break lines even though this line exceeds ideal code length. It only breaks line when the logical paragraph ends like here.

The prompt ends with a line break and then calls strip().
""".strip()
```

❌ Bad: Indented prompt (wastes tokens and harms readability)
```python
bad_example_1 = """
    This prompt is indented.
    This adds unnecessary tokens and should never be done.
""".strip()
```

❌ Bad: Implicit string concatenation
```python
bad_example_2 = (
    "This prompt is split across strings "
    "which makes it harder to edit and review."
)
```

❌ Bad: Line breaks inserted only to satisfy code width
```python
bad_example_3 = """
This paragraph is broken into multiple lines even though it is a single logical paragraph. This is
done only to keep code lines short, which is incorrect because the prompt is sent to the LLM
exactly as written and these artificial breaks degrade clarity and quality.
""".strip()
```
